Answer: When a class is declared as final, no other class can inherit from it. When a method is declared as final, a child class cannot override that method. When a variable is declared as final, its value cannot be reassigned after it has been initialized. A class is normally made final when it should not have any subclasses. In an inheritance hierarchy, leaf classes can be declared as final. For example:

```java
final class HomeSparrow extends Sparrow {
}
```

Another class cannot extend HomeSparrow.

Answer: A final method cannot be overridden in a child class. It is used when the implementation of a method should remain unchanged in subclasses. For example:

```java
class Demo {
public final void display() {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
}
class Demo1 extends Demo {
public void display() {
System.out.println("Hello World");
}
}
```

The display() method in Demo1 produces a compilation error because the method in Demo is final. A final method can still be overloaded. Method overloading means defining multiple methods with the same name but different signatures. For example:

```java
class Demo {
final void display() {
System.out.println("Hello");
}
final void display(int a) {
System.out.println(a);
}
}
```

The methods are overloaded because their parameter lists are different.

Answer: A final variable cannot be assigned a new value after it has been initialized. For example:

```java
class Sample {
public static void main(String[] args) {
final int a = 10;
System.out.println(a);
a = 20;
System.out.println(a);
}
}
```

The statement a = 20; causes a compilation error because a has already been initialized. A final local variable can be declared first and initialized later:

```java
final int a;
a = 20;
```

This is valid because the variable is initialized only once. A final instance variable cannot be left uninitialized in its declaration and assigned arbitrarily later through a method. It must be properly initialized as part of instance initialization, such as through its declaration or constructor. For example:

```java
class Sample {
final int a = 100;
}
```

After initialization, a cannot be reassigned.

Answer: A local variable belongs to a method and is created when the method is called. A static variable belongs to the class and is created when the class is loaded. Since a variable declared inside a method is always a local variable, it cannot be declared as static. This rule is the same whether the method itself is static or instance-based. For example, the following concept is not valid:

```java
static void display() {
static int a = 10;
}
```

The variable a is a local variable because it is declared inside the method, so it cannot be static.

Answer: An instance method is called using an object reference.

```java
Demo d = new Demo();
d.display();
```

A static method is normally called using the class name.

```java
Demo.display();
```

A static method can technically be called using an object reference, but this is not the standard way of calling it. An instance method cannot be called directly using the class name. The purpose of making a method static is to allow it to be called without creating an object.

Answer: Yes. A method with a void return type can contain a return statement, but it cannot return a value. For example: void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Hello");
return;
}
```

The return statement simply terminates the method. The following is invalid: void display() {

```java
return 10;
}
```

A void method cannot return a value.

Answer: Object-oriented programming is a programming paradigm used to solve real-world problems by representing entities as objects. A class is a blueprint given to the JVM for creating objects. An object is an instance of a class and represents the physical existence of the class in memory. For example:

```java
class Student {
String name;
}
Student s = new Student();
```

Here, Student is the class and s refers to an object created from that class.

Answer: A variable should be static when its value is common to all objects of the class. A variable should be instance-based when its value is specific to an individual object. A variable can be declared as static final when its value is common to the class and should not change. For example:

```java
class College {
static final int FEES = 50000;
}
```

The static keyword provides one common class-level copy, while final prevents the value from being changed. A static variable by itself is not a constant. The unchangeable property comes from final.

Answer: A method is declared as static when it can be called without creating an object. Generic operations that do not depend on a particular object's state can be implemented as static methods. Static methods are commonly called utility methods. For example:

```java
class MathUtility {
static int add(int a, int b) {
return a + b;
}
}
```

The method can be called as:

```java
int result = MathUtility.add(10, 20);
```

Answer: A static block is used when a group of statements must execute during class initialization before the main() method executes. It can also be used to initialize static variables. For example:

```java
class Demo {
static int a;
static {
a = 10;
System.out.println("Static block executed");
}
public static void main(String[] args) {
System.out.println(a);
}
}
```

A static block is also called a static initializer.

