If no constructor is explicitly defined in a class, the compiler provides a zero-parameter constructor called the default constructor. The default constructor contains a call to the parent class constructor.

Answer: this() is used to call another constructor of the same class. super() is used to call the constructor of the immediate parent class. A constructor begins with a constructor invocation involving this() or super(). For example:

```java
class Student {
```

Student() {

```java
this(20);
System.out.println("No-argument constructor");
}
```

Student(int age) {

```java
System.out.println(age);
}
}
```

Here, this(20) calls another constructor of the same class. This is called local constructor chaining. When a child constructor calls the parent constructor using super(), it is constructor chaining between the child and parent classes.

Answer: Shadowing occurs when a local variable or parameter has the same name as an instance variable. For example:

```java
class Student {
int age;
```

Student(int age) {

```java
age = age;
}
}
```

Here, both age references refer to the constructor parameter. The instance variable is not properly assigned. The this keyword represents the current object and is used to distinguish the instance variable from the parameter.

```java
class Student {
int age;
```

Student(int age) {

```java
this.age = age;
}
}
```

Here, this.age refers to the instance variable, while age refers to the constructor parameter.

Answer: Encapsulation is the binding of data members and methods into a single unit while providing controlled access to the data. Encapsulation is achieved using both: Private data members Getter and setter methods For example:

```java
class Student {
private int age;
public void setAge(int age) {
this.age = age;
}
public int getAge() {
return age;
}
}
```

The private keyword restricts direct access to the variable, while getter and setter methods provide controlled access. The main advantage of encapsulation is data security.

Answer: Inheritance is the mechanism through which properties and behavior are acquired from a parent class by a child class. The parent class is also called: Superclass Base class The child class is also called: Subclass Derived class The extends keyword is used to perform class inheritance. For example:

```java
class Parent {
}
class Child extends Parent {
}
```

Answer: When an object of a child class is created, the parent class constructor is also executed through constructor chaining. For example:

```java
class Parent {
```

Parent() {

```java
System.out.println("Parent");
}
}
class Child extends Parent {
```

Child() {

```java
System.out.println("Child");
}
}
class Demo {
public static void main(String[] args) {
Child c = new Child();
}
}
```

Output: Parent Child The parent constructor executes before the child constructor. In a multilevel hierarchy, constructor execution proceeds from the top-level parent toward the child. For example: Object | Class1 | Class2 | Class3 | Class4 When a Class4 object is created, Class4 calls Class3, Class3 calls Class2, Class2 calls Class1, and Class1 ultimately calls the Object constructor.

Answer: From the perspective of inheritance, methods can be classified into three types: Inherited methods Overridden methods Specialized methods An inherited method is acquired from the parent class and used by the child without changing its implementation. An overridden method is inherited from the parent class but its implementation is changed in the child according to the child's requirement. A specialized method is present only in the child class and is not present in the parent class. For example:

```java
class Parent {
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Parent");
}
}
class Child extends Parent {
@Override
```

void display() {

```java
System.out.println("Child");
}
```

void specialOperation() {

```java
System.out.println("Special operation");
}
}
```

Here: display() is an overridden method. The inherited display() from Parent is changed in Child. specialOperation() is a specialized method because it exists only in Child.
